# Data — the raw corpus is 3.4 GB, never commit it.
# The leading slash matters: an unanchored `data/` also matches src/qar/data/,
# which silently excluded the whole corpus-preparation package from the repo.
*.jsonl
/data/

# Experiment outputs. Keep everything a run *learned*; drop everything it weighs.
#
# `/runs/*` and not `/runs/`: git never descends into an excluded *directory*, so a
# later `!` rule could not re-include anything beneath it. Excluding the contents
# instead leaves the directory visible and the negations below effective.
/runs/*
!/runs/*/
# What travels (~400 KB total): the results tables every reported number is read
# from, the metrics.jsonl training curves the report plots — which cannot be
# regenerated without re-running the training — and each run's resolved config,
# which is its provenance.
#
# What does not: checkpoints, at ~550 MB each and 9.9 GB across ten runs. GitHub
# rejects single files over 100 MB, and a checkpoint is only needed to *re-evaluate*
# a model. Every model trained so far has already been evaluated and its numbers are
# in runs/_baselines/, so losing them costs nothing unless a checkpoint is wanted for
# a measurement not yet taken — the test split being the obvious one. Copy those by
# hand, outside git.
/runs/*/checkpoints/
# metrics.jsonl would otherwise be caught by the corpus-wide `*.jsonl` rule above.
# Last match wins in a .gitignore, so this negation has to live below it.
!/runs/*/metrics.jsonl
checkpoints/
*.pt
*.ckpt
*.faiss
*.index

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Tooling
.env
.vscode/
.idea/
